window.docContents[250].resolve({"/#monad-do-notation":{"contents":"While APIs based on monads are very powerful, the explicit use of >>= with anonymous functions is still somewhat noisy.\nJust as infix operators are used instead of explicit calls to , Lean provides a syntax for monads called do-notation that can make programs that use monads easier to read and write.\nThis is the very same do-notation that is used to write programs in , and  is also a monad.\n\nIn Hello, World!, the do syntax is used to combine  actions, but the meaning of these programs is explained directly.\nUnderstanding how to program with monads means that do can now be explained in terms of how it translates into uses of the underlying monad operators.\n\nThe first translation of do is used when the only statement in the do is a single expression .\nIn this case, the do is removed, so\n\n\n\ntranslates to\n\n\n\nThe second translation is used when the first statement of the do is a let with an arrow, binding a local variable.\nThis translates to a use of >>= together with a function that binds that very same variable, so\n\n\n\ntranslates to\n\n\n\nWhen the first statement of the do block is an expression, then it is considered to be a monadic action that returns , so the function matches the  constructor and\n\n\n\ntranslates to\n\n\n\nFinally, when the first statement of the do block is a let that uses :=, the translated form is an ordinary let expression, so\n\n\n\ntranslates to\n\n\n\nThe definition of  that uses the  class looks like this:Using do-notation, it becomes significantly more readable:\n\nWithout the  type class, the function  that numbers the nodes of a tree was written:With  and do, its definition is much less noisy:\n\nAll of the conveniences from do with  are also available when using it with other monads.\nFor example, nested actions also work in any monad.\nThe original definition of  was:\n\n\n\nWith do-notation, it can be written:\n\n\n\nUsing nested actions makes it almost as short as the original non-monadic :\n\n\n\nUsing nested actions,  can be made much more concise:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads","header":"4.4. do -Notation for Monads","id":"/#monad-do-notation"},"/#positive-number-coercion":{"contents":"Every positive number corresponds to a natural number.\nThe function  that was defined earlier converts a  to the corresponding :\n\n\n\nThe function , with type , removes a prefix of a list.\nApplying  to a , however, leads to a type error:\n\n\n\n\n\nBecause the author of  did not make it a method of a type class, it can't be overridden by defining a new instance.\n\nThe type class  describes overloaded ways of coercing from one type to another:An instance of  is enough to allow the prior code to work:Using #check shows the result of the instance search that was used behind the scenes:\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Coercions","header":"3.6.2. Positive Numbers","id":"/#positive-number-coercion"},"/#structure-inheritance":{"contents":"In order to understand the full definitions of , , and , another Lean feature is necessary: structure inheritance.\nStructure inheritance allows one structure type to provide the interface of another, along with additional fields.\nThis can be useful when modeling concepts that have a clear taxonomic relationship.\nFor example, take a model of mythical creatures.\nSome of them are large, and some are small:\n\n\n\nBehind the scenes, defining the  structure creates an inductive type with a single constructor called :\n\n\n\n\n\nSimilarly, a function  is created that actually extracts the field from the constructor:\n\n\n\n\n\nIn most old stories, each monster can be defeated in some way.\nA description of a monster should include this information, along with whether it is large:\n\n\n\nThe  in the heading states that every monster is also mythical.\nTo define a , both the fields from  and the fields from  should be provided.\nA troll is a large monster that is vulnerable to sunlight:\n\n\n\nBehind the scenes, inheritance is implemented using composition.\nThe constructor  takes a  as its argument:\n\n\n\n\n\nIn addition to defining functions to extract the value of each new field, a function  is defined with type .\nThis can be used to extract the underlying creature.\n\nMoving up the inheritance hierarchy in Lean is not the same thing as upcasting in object-oriented languages.\nAn upcast operator causes a value from a derived class to be treated as an instance of the parent class, but the value retains its identity and structure.\nIn Lean, however, moving up the inheritance hierarchy actually erases the underlying information.\nTo see this in action, consider the result of evaluating :\n\n\n\n\n\nOnly the fields of  remain.\n\nJust like the where syntax, curly-brace notation with field names also works with structure inheritance:\n\n\n\nHowever, the anonymous angle-bracket notation that delegates to the underlying constructor reveals the internal details:\n\n\n\n\n\nAn extra set of angle brackets is required, which invokes  on :\n\n\n\nLean's dot notation is capable of taking inheritance into account.\nIn other words, the existing  can be used with a , and Lean automatically inserts the call to  before the call to .\nHowever, this only occurs when using dot notation, and applying the field lookup function using normal function call syntax results in a type error:\n\n\n\n\n\nDot notation can also take inheritance into account for user-defined functions.\nA small creature is one that is not large:\n\n\n\nEvaluating  yields , while attempting to evaluate  results in:\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads","header":"5.1. Structures and Inheritance","id":"/#structure-inheritance"}});